        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          Fourth episode in the series. The first set out what a management rhythm actually is, the second cut the dashboard down to the indicators that genuinely trigger a decision, the third kept a record of what had been settled. What remains is the journey: how does a priority decided at the top reach the people who will do the work?
        </p>

        <p>
          The figure framing this episode comes from McKinsey's <em>The State of Organizations 2026</em> survey of more than 10,000 senior leaders. <strong>56% of executive committee members say they are clear on their organisation's must-win priorities. At middle management level, only 27% are.</strong> Half as many. The gap is not about the quality of the strategy: it is about what happens to it on the way down.
        </p>

        <h2>You have already announced them three times</h2>

        <p>
          No executive team keeps its priorities to itself. Three mechanisms exist almost everywhere, and all three are sound reflexes. Each one stops short of the front line.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The start-of-year seminar</strong>: the strategy presented to everyone, in a single day, properly resourced. Everyone leaves with the deck. <strong>The annual objectives letter</strong>: each department receives its own, in writing, in black and white. It is signed in January and reopened in December. <strong>The cascade in team meetings</strong>: each manager passes the message on to their team — in their own words, and with whatever they retained of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          What the three have in common fits in one sentence: <strong>priorities are transmitted once a year, while the work itself is decided every week.</strong> Between two announcements, hundreds of trade-offs are made that nobody connects back to anything.
        </p>

        <h2>What gets lost on the way down</h2>

        <p>
          Nobody distorts the message deliberately. It simply crosses too many levels, and three mechanisms work against it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Everyone rephrases at their own level.</strong> The message passes through three relays, and each one restates it in their own words. What arrives at the bottom is the third version, and nobody has ever compared that version with the original.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The priority arrives without a date.</strong> "Improve turnaround times" gets filed under "important". The file on today's desk, by contrast, carries a date. Between the two, the calendar decides on its own.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>This morning wins.</strong> This is the decisive mechanism, and the most ordinary one: between the priority of the year and the urgency of this morning, urgency wins every time. Each of those wins is reasonable taken on its own. Strung together, they fill the entire year.
        </p>

        <p>
          The effect shows up in the accounts. A part everyone knows must be replaced, ordered three months ahead instead of six, carries an emergency premium that is anything but marginal. The objective "keep procurement under control" did exist, and it was the right one. It had simply never become a date in anyone's calendar.
        </p>

        <h2>Four things, and the priority reaches the bottom</h2>

        <p>
          What follows requires no tool, no project and no additional committee — which is precisely the point. A priority travels downward when it becomes four concrete things.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>① A sentence the level below can repeat.</strong> The test is immediate: if your department head can say it from memory, it will keep travelling. If they have to reopen a document to find it, it stops with them.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>② A number they already see.</strong> Use a figure they have in front of them every week, rather than creating one for the occasion. A new indicator has to be fed; an existing one only has to be read differently.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>③ A date in their calendar.</strong> A specific day when you look together at where things stand — not "this year", not "at the next review". A priority without an appointment loses to any file that has one.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>④ Something they stop doing.</strong> This is the one that always gets skipped, and it is the one that makes room for the other three. A priority that removes nothing is simply added to an already full pile, and the pile has seniority on its side.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-1">Our advice</p>
          <p class="text-blue-900">You have already presented your priorities, and you were right to do so: without that presentation, nobody would know where the company is heading. What is missing lies elsewhere. Take a single priority — the most important of the three. Go and see a single department head. Decide together what they stop doing in order to hold it. One priority brought down properly travels further than twelve announced together.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-1">Diagnostic question</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Ask a department head to name your three priorities for the year, without notes. Then open their calendar for the next two weeks: how many hours carry those priorities?</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Why this matters in New Caledonia</h2>

        <p>
          Activity has picked up in New Caledonia, but the teams carrying it are leaner than they were two years ago. When there are fewer middle managers, every relay matters more: a priority that stops at one level no longer finds anyone to pick it up at the next. That is exactly the situation in which the journey of an objective matters as much as its content.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your strategy probably does not need rewriting. It needs a relay that holds all the way down — and that is a far shorter piece of work.
        </p>

